  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nIssued at 153 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nSunday, a strong 597-dam 500-mb upper-level ridge will remain \ncentered across the south-central United States, while a trough \nremains across the western United States. This will maintain broad \nwest to southwest flow aloft across the region. Warm conditions will \npersist as 850-mb temperatures remain in the 25 to 30C range, \nsupporting afternoon highs generally in the lower to middle 90s. \nDespite somewhat stronger flow aloft, precipitation chances should \nremain relatively low Sunday as warm mid-level temperatures and \nassociated capping limit convective development. Overnight \ntemperatures will remain quite mild, with lows generally in the \nupper 60s to lower 70s.\n\nMonday, the upper-level ridge is forecast to flatten and shift \nfarther into the southeastern United States as a weak shortwave \nejects northeastward from the western longwave trough. An associated \nfrontal boundary is expected to move southeast across the region \nduring the day, bringing cooler temperatures with highs ranging from \nthe middle 80s north to upper 80s south. Increasing large-scale \nforcing and low-level convergence along the boundary should also \nresult in increasing shower and thunderstorm chances Monday \nafternoon into the evening. Severe weather may be possible given the \npotential overlap of sufficient instability and stronger deep-layer \nshear. However, confidence in the magnitude and location of the \nthreat remains low at this range.\n\nTuesday into late next week, the western United States trough will \nattempt to progress eastward while the upper-level ridge across the \nsoutheastern United States begins to reamplify and expand westward. \nThis evolution should maintain relatively warm conditions, with \nhighs returning to around 90 degrees or the lower 90s by the middle \nand latter portions of next week. Periodic precipitation chances \nwill remain possible, though the timing and coverage will depend \nheavily on the strength and track of individual disturbances moving \nthrough the flow. Overall, the long-term pattern favors the better \nopportunities for showers and thunderstorms through the weekend into \nearly next week, followed by a trend toward warmer and drier \nconditions by Wednesday into the latter part of next week.",
